<?xml version="1.0"?>
<ScenContainer xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance" xmlns:xsd="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema">
  <ScenTitle>Peeling the Onion, 1957</ScenTitle>
  <ScenDescription>&lt;BODY scroll=auto&gt;&lt;P&gt;&lt;FONT color=red&gt;Scenario Version 1.2 Upgraded for CMO v1.03 Build 1147.25&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;&lt;FONT color=#ff0000&gt;Aircraft Damage is activated.&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;SAC vs. the Soviet Union&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;You are Commander, &lt;EM&gt;308th Bombardment Wing, Strategic Air Command &lt;/EM&gt;equipped with B-47E medium bombers and&amp;nbsp;normally based at Hunter Air Force Base (AFB), outside Savannah, GA. You are also the current OP REFLEX Commander&amp;nbsp;on temporary duty at&amp;nbsp;Sidi Slimane AFB in French Morocco&amp;nbsp;and in the event of war will be responsible to conduct nuclear strikes against the Soviet Union out of the &lt;EM&gt;SAC&lt;/EM&gt; forward&amp;nbsp;bases: Sidi Slimane with elements of your combat wing, Ben Guerir AFB (&lt;EM&gt;19th Bombardment Wing&amp;nbsp;&lt;/EM&gt;with B-47's) and Nouasseur AFB&amp;nbsp;(&lt;EM&gt;72nd Bombardment Wing &lt;/EM&gt;with B-36's). You have the REFLEX elements of&amp;nbsp;these combat wings&amp;nbsp;in addition to your own forces and the stateside Wing Commanders will&amp;nbsp;allocate their bombers to your command&amp;nbsp;from bases in the USA as they become ready.&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;Hostilities with the Soviet Union have not commenced but if&amp;nbsp;they do, your mission will be to&amp;nbsp;attack Soviet&amp;nbsp;industrial targets&amp;nbsp;starting&amp;nbsp;in&amp;nbsp;southern Russia on the Black Sea coast. You have over flight rights from Italy, Greece and Turkey but Western European airspace belongs to the &lt;EM&gt;SAC&lt;/EM&gt; REFLEX forces in the UK and Spain, the &lt;EM&gt;RAF, &lt;/EM&gt;NATO&amp;nbsp;and &lt;EM&gt;Tactical Air Command&lt;/EM&gt;. Other &lt;EM&gt;SAC&lt;/EM&gt; Wings will operate from south Asia east from the Caucasus mountains. You have no fighter escorts (in &lt;EM&gt;SAC&lt;/EM&gt;, the bomber will always get through) and the NATO allies have no significant assets or support to give you outside of the bases at Lajes AFB in the Azores, Gioia del Colle AFB in Italy and RAF Akrotiri on Cyprus for refueling. Assigned to your command are the &lt;EM&gt;3rd Reconnaissance&amp;nbsp;Squadron &lt;/EM&gt;(12 x RB-47) of the &lt;EM&gt;26th Strategic Reconnaissance Wing &lt;/EM&gt;and a detachment&amp;nbsp;of EC-121 Warning Stars from the &lt;EM&gt;961st Airborne Electronic Warning and Control Squadron&lt;/EM&gt; for airborne command and control.&lt;SPAN style="WHITE-SPACE: normal; WORD-SPACING: 0px; TEXT-TRANSFORM: none; FLOAT: none; COLOR: rgb(37,37,37); FONT: 14px/22px sans-serif; WIDOWS: 1; DISPLAY: inline !important; LETTER-SPACING: normal; BACKGROUND-COLOR: rgb(255,255,255); TEXT-INDENT: 0px; -webkit-text-stroke-width: 0px"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/SPAN&gt;There are quantities of&amp;nbsp;nuclear weapons stockpiled at&amp;nbsp;your Moroccan bases and more will be flown in as necessary by MATS.&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;War&amp;nbsp;could be&amp;nbsp;just hours away...&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;&lt;STRONG&gt;IMPORTANT&lt;/STRONG&gt; - Scenario background notes. Please read before playing:&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;The United States had no national plan to conduct nuclear war against the Soviet Union until the summer of 1961 when SIOP-62 (Single Integrated Operational Plan for F/Y 1962) was formally adopted by the Kennedy administration. During the Eisenhower years the nuclear equipped services each essentially went their own way, selecting their own targets and methods of attack. There was little or no coordination and the likely result of a nuclear&amp;nbsp;exchange would have been command and control chaos. None of the services effectively&amp;nbsp;shared intelligence, each maintained their own target lists and associated targeting priorities and there was no effective cooperation at the all-important operational command&amp;nbsp;level. In a 1957&amp;nbsp;Joint Chief's of Staff&amp;nbsp;conference hosted by &lt;EM&gt;SAC&lt;/EM&gt; over 300 targeting&amp;nbsp;conflicts with the Army, Navy and Tactical Air Force were identified and virtually none of them had been addressed one-year later.&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;A&amp;nbsp;principle architect of this unsatisfactory situation was probably Air Force General Curtis LeMay, commander of the &lt;EM&gt;Strategic Air Command&lt;/EM&gt; (&lt;EM&gt;SAC&lt;/EM&gt;) until&amp;nbsp;30 June&amp;nbsp;1957 when he was appointed Vice-Chief of Staff of the USAF. LeMay had built &lt;EM&gt;SAC&lt;/EM&gt; into a formidable combat force with the best available equipment and a very rigorous and realistic training regime coupled with a&amp;nbsp;personel selection program aimed at ensuring that only the best joined the Command. LeMay was entirely a bomber man; he fought&amp;nbsp;Chief of Naval Operations&amp;nbsp;Admiral Arleigh Burke&amp;nbsp;by trying to&amp;nbsp;discredit and scuttle the&amp;nbsp;Polaris SLBM, publicly scoffed at the Army’s Redstone IRBM project and tactical nuclear weapons like Honest John and tried to actively undermine&amp;nbsp;air force&amp;nbsp;Brigadier General Bernard Schriever’s &lt;EM&gt;Missile Development Center&lt;/EM&gt;&amp;nbsp;resposible for&amp;nbsp;the Atlas and Titan ICBM programs. LeMay has sometimes been characterized as a madman but probably part of the problem is that he was&amp;nbsp;often the smartest man in the room, at least in his opinion. That said, by 1957 his mind was essentially closed to any offensive weapon other than manned bombers and he was&amp;nbsp;completely opposed to sharing the burden of fighting the Soviets in a nuclear war with the other services particularly the Navy, for&amp;nbsp;which he&amp;nbsp;repeatedly demonstrated unconcealed contempt. He was&amp;nbsp;convinced that only the United States Air Force should possess strategic nuclear weapons, that nuclear war with the Soviet Union was inevitable and that &lt;EM&gt;SAC&lt;/EM&gt; was the only instrument that could win it when it came.&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;LeMay’s hand-picked replacement as Commander in Chief, Strategic Air Command (CINCSAC) was General Thomas Power, another of the USAF’s “Bomber Barons”&amp;nbsp;but one who&amp;nbsp;had identified issues in the Air Force plans to actually deliver nuclear weapons to targets in the Soviet Union with the forces available. Power was a hard task-master like LeMay but more pragmatic and&amp;nbsp;inclined to accept that the ICBM would eventually become&amp;nbsp;a viable nuclear delivery system&amp;nbsp;so active resistance from&amp;nbsp;CINCSAC to the Atlas, Titan and Minuteman programs soon tapered off. Until the missiles arrived, the bombers still had to&amp;nbsp;go it alone&amp;nbsp;and in accordance with LeMay’s dictum that America had just 15-minutes to launch its first strike after warning or attack, &lt;EM&gt;SAC&lt;/EM&gt; greatly expanded its alert posture, airborne alerts and forward basing. By the summer of 1957 some 11% of &lt;EM&gt;SAC &lt;/EM&gt;bombers were loaded with their nuclear payloads at 15-minutes alert for takeoff or flying airborne alerts around North America with the eventual goal being&amp;nbsp;one-third of the force. In addition, forward &lt;EM&gt;SAC&lt;/EM&gt; bases in Alaska,&amp;nbsp;Europe and French Morocco, thousands of miles closer to Russia than the bases in the CONUS saw significant forces rotated through at 90-day intervals (30-days in the Alaska command). These&amp;nbsp;forward deployments were designated OPERATION REFLEX ACTION and generally&amp;nbsp;around five or six bombers plus tanker support from&amp;nbsp;the duty combat wings would deploy to Morocco, Spain and the UK.&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;OP REFLEX moved bombers closer to the Soviet Union but they still had several thousand miles to fly to reach targets of any importance. &lt;EM&gt;SAC&lt;/EM&gt; anticipated&amp;nbsp;conducting continuous attacks over a period of several days during which some 2,500 megatons would land on Russian strategic targets, destroying the war-making capability of the Soviet Union. The advent of jet interceptors and the radar-guided surface to air missile also meant that bomber survivability had become a real concern. With deep penetration missions becoming increasingly problematic, the bomber force would begin by attacking targets on the peripheries of the Soviet Union, concentrating on air defence assets as a priority and once the skies were cleared of Russian fighters, the way would be&amp;nbsp;open for deeper raids against Soviet industrial infrastructure - cities. This&amp;nbsp;rolling back defensive layers was likened to the peeling of an onion, layer by layer. It might take four-days to get the bombers to Moscow but everything of value on the path to the capital would be a radioactive wasteland by then. If the Soviet leadership evacuated the cities to save lives, so much the better: the factories would still stop production before the bombs hit and the starving population driven into the countryside would rise up in revolution to end the war. This was the essence of the doctrine of Massive Retaliation and Curtis LeMay’s vision for winning a nuclear war.&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;You have three &lt;EM&gt;SAC&lt;/EM&gt; combat wings and their associated&amp;nbsp;tanker forces to try.&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;******************************************************************&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;&amp;nbsp;Scenario Designer's Entirely Optional and Far Too Verbose Notes:&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;EM&gt;Peeling the Onion&lt;/EM&gt;&amp;nbsp;is an attempt to use &lt;STRONG&gt;CMANO&lt;/STRONG&gt; to recreate the problems facing &lt;EM&gt;SAC&lt;/EM&gt; in trying to carry out nuclear strikes against the Soviet Union using only manned bombers and gravity bombs. There are as yet no stand-off munitions and the main enemy of the bomber will be cannon armed interceptors although by September 1957, the SA-2 Guideline (S-75) SAM was being deployed around high-value real estate in Russia and the first generation of air-to-air missiles were entering service. The distances that &lt;EM&gt;SAC&lt;/EM&gt; planned to operate over were vast and it was essentially impossible to conduct a single massed attack in the style of WW2. Nor was stealth a viable option as the Soviet Union was ringed with radar stations and fighter bases of the &lt;EM&gt;PVO&lt;/EM&gt;, the arm of the Red Air Force tasked with air defence. This scenario is partly an exercise in aviation logistics: your best asset is the B-47 but it is tied to the very slow KC-97 tanker force. By this time there were a handful of KC-135 jet tankers (the first deliveries begin in June 1957) and&amp;nbsp;a few&amp;nbsp;B-52 combat wings were on alert but the B-47 medium bomber made up the core of &lt;EM&gt;SAC's&lt;/EM&gt; power.&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;Large areas of the map are Navigation Zones which you are prevented from entering. This is by design since the airspace will be needed by other units and there is no attempt to model this war outside of the Player's area of responsibility. There was actually five &lt;EM&gt;SAC &lt;/EM&gt;forward bases in Morocco but I have elected to use only three. You have three full combat wings and each has a designated forward base but you may freely mix and match&amp;nbsp;squadron and wing assets&amp;nbsp;as desired. You may find that you have a shortage of tankers; deal with it as best you can because at this time &lt;EM&gt;SAC&lt;/EM&gt; had&amp;nbsp;more than two&amp;nbsp;bombers&amp;nbsp;for every&amp;nbsp;tanker and the latter were always a big delimiter during operations. Your only real combat support is a squadron of RB-47's, initially at Lajes AFB&amp;nbsp;and a detachment of EC-121 Warning Stars to provide airborne radar coverage.&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;Your bases&amp;nbsp;have no air defenses or radars for the simple reason that the Soviets have nothing that can reach you or rather anything that might be capable of hitting Morocco probably has more urgent targets. This represents the reality behind the Bomber Gap&amp;nbsp;propaganda that Congress and the Senate used to spend ever more taxpayer dollars on nuclear arms. Russian bombers could not effectively hit any American targets outside of Europe or Alaska which is why they were expending so much effort to deploy&amp;nbsp;an ICBM.&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;Your goal is to destroy Soviet industry, which&amp;nbsp;in the jargon of nuclear war&amp;nbsp;is known as countervalue targeting. Most of us know these types of targets as cities and you will receive victory points (VP) for eliminating them. You will lose VP for losing bombers, not because a bomber is worth a city but rather losses will probably impair your ability to penetrate deep into the Soviet industrial heartland in the time provided. Most of your target cities are made up several markers&amp;nbsp;and or high value&amp;nbsp;facilities and not all of these may be visible at the start of the scenario.&amp;nbsp;You get nothing for killing military&amp;nbsp;assets as doing so is a necessary prerequisite for surviving attacks on your primary targets. Expect target cities to be ringed with AAA (often 100mm guns), perhaps&amp;nbsp;some SAMs and fighter bases. &lt;EM&gt;SAC&lt;/EM&gt; planners anticipated hitting the &lt;EM&gt;PVO&lt;/EM&gt; bases first, thus opening the way to attack the cities. You're obviously free to try something else. The Soviet Black Sea coast is very heavily defended and units of the Black Sea Fleet may be at sea acting as radar pickets. As with my&amp;nbsp;&lt;EM&gt;Wargasm, 1962&lt;/EM&gt; the scenario includes separate target information in printable *.rtf format.&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;A word about your base magazines. The &lt;EM&gt;SAC&lt;/EM&gt; forward bases included only limited quantities of "special weapons" with their associated storage,&amp;nbsp;testing, repair and security infrastructure. America possessed well over 15,000 deliverable nuclear weapons by the summer of 1957 and the numbers sent overseas is still classified and probably varied widely. The scenario presumes that the MATS will be flying replacement bombs to your bases as fast as you use them so you should not run out of nuclear ordnance but may run short of certain weapons. A complex system of events and scripts was initially tried to force the Player to run transport flights across the Atlantic but it proved unsatisfactory, added nothing to the scenario and was deleted early in development.&amp;nbsp;Some of the references consulted indicate that special combat wing headquarters were established to command the OP REFLEX forward-based assets. I have chosen to&amp;nbsp;designate the Player in this role as one of the duty wings providing the bombers and tankers since the actual combat wings and HQ designation for Moroccan forward bases for September 1957 were unclear. The home bases and makeup of the &lt;EM&gt;19th&lt;/EM&gt;, &lt;EM&gt;72nd&lt;/EM&gt; and &lt;EM&gt;308th Bombardment Wings &lt;/EM&gt;should be accurate for the scenario dates.&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;Based upon your orders and the target information provided your task is to wipe out the industrial capacity of the Soviet Union within your AOR. Only two missions have been provided; Support missions representing the Positive Control Points (PCP), orbited by bombers and sometimes attending tankers, until the Go message was received. At that point the bombers would begin their attack mission profiles, usually&amp;nbsp;with full tanks. In popular jargon these were referred to as "Fail-Safe" points but it does not appear that &lt;EM&gt;SAC&lt;/EM&gt; ever formally used that term.&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;About this war that never was. Mid-1957 was a period of great tensions between the USA and the USSR. America had begun U-2 overflights of the Soviet Union, something that enraged Moscow while the latter was&amp;nbsp;expending vast sums to get a working ICBM into service. America's space program was a series of highly embarrassing if spectactular flops and lacking an intercontinental missile, the USAF was working with the UK, Turkey and Italy to take intermediate-ranged missiles, Thor for Britain and Jupiter for the other two. Nuclear weapons had no permissive action links to prevent unauthorized use and were not as yet one-point safe; a capability that prevented unwanted functioning of the fission&amp;nbsp;mechanisms &amp;nbsp;in the event of an accident.&amp;nbsp;These were much more common than the public knew and on several occasions a catastrophic nuclear event was avoided only by the narrowest of margins. As for hostilities, at some point an event will fire and war will begin; there is no attempt to model who started it, why or how it begin as these are entirely irrelevant to carrying out your orders. In the meantime, for SAC's alert forces it's just another day.&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;&amp;nbsp;Hopefully you have your work cut out for you and can perhaps spare a thought for the alert crews of &lt;EM&gt;SAC&lt;/EM&gt; and the &lt;EM&gt;PVO&lt;/EM&gt; who trained for this very scenario and anticipated that at any time a global nuclear war could begin.&amp;nbsp;Fortunately it never did and &lt;EM&gt;Peeling the Onion&lt;/EM&gt; is just an exercise in fantasy. Thank you&amp;nbsp;for playing; please post questions or problems in the Matrix &lt;STRONG&gt;CMANO&lt;/STRONG&gt; Forum.&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;Christopher Comars, aka Randomizer&lt;/P&gt;&lt;/BODY&gt;</ScenDescription>
